  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Smoke continues to affect the region. \n\n- There is a chance for showers today in Northwest Montana mainly\n  east of Highway 93 and along the Continental Divide. \n\n- Today starts a warming trend with a dry air mass in place.\n\nHigh resolution models show the smoke spreading across more of\nwestern Montana and central Idaho early this morning. Kalispell\nshould see some relief due to a shift to northerly winds overnight\npushing much of the smoke further south. However, wildland fires\nupstream in Washington and some local fires will keep producing\nsmoke even there. \n\nA shortwave moving across northwest Montana this morning has a 20%\nchance of causing some light showers in Kalispell, and those\nchances increase east of Highway 93 and peak at 60% along the\nContinental Divide. Other than that, the air mass over the region\nremains dry. \n\nRising heights will cause warming temperatures today, even though\nthe flow aloft remains cyclonic. By tomorrow, the flow will start\nto turn anticyclonic, and the warming trend will continue with\nmore stable conditions. Unfortunately this means locally produced\nsmoke will be less likely to ventilate out as the week goes on.\nAlso, with the dry air mass over the region, that means the\nhumidity will stay very low this week, exasperating the dryness\nhelping to fuel wildland fire. Fortunately, there doesn't seem to\nbe anymore lightning on the way this week. Basically, the region\nhas a warm, dry, smoky week ahead.",
